from itertools import product
from edtrace import text
from lesson_demos import (STORIES, story, outcome_demo, budget_demo, vote_demo,
                          selection_demo, matching_demo, horizon_demo)


def main():

    text('## 01 · Agent Evaluation and Benchmarks')
    text('CSE 398/498 · Vibe Research for Scientific Discovery · Lichao Sun · Lehigh University')
    text('In earlier classes, we saw how an agent chooses actions, calls tools, and uses the results to decide what to do next. That helps us build an agent. Today we ask how to check the work it produces.')
    text('Imagine that your project includes a support agent. You show a successful booking cancellation in a demo. Before someone uses it, they will want to know whether it canceled the right booking, followed the rules, and can do the same job again.')
    text('**How do we move from one convincing demo to a result someone else can check?** This question connects all the papers in this lecture.')
    text('An evaluation runs a task and checks the result. A benchmark provides a collection of tasks and shared scoring rules. Those rules let us count successes and compare systems.')
    text('We will start with the evidence for one successful task. Then we will ask what else we need before claiming that one agent is better than another.')

    text('## 02 · Today’s Plan')
    text('Suppose we want to report that our agent is better than a baseline. A higher percentage is the end of the argument. First we need to explain what was tested and why that percentage means something.')
    text('- **Start with one task.**\n\n  We must decide what counts as success before counting successful runs. The booking example and *τ²-Bench* show how task rules and repeated attempts change the question we are answering.')
    text('- **Compare two systems.**\n\n  Once we can score a run, we can compare designs. *AI Agents That Matter* asks whether a higher score comes from a better method, more attempts, or a larger budget.')
    text('- **Read rankings and check graders.**\n\n  Some results have no single answer that a program can check. We will see how *Chatbot Arena* uses human votes, then ask what happens when a model does the grading.')
    text('- **Choose tasks that support the claim.**\n\n  A system may answer hard questions but struggle with a sequence of actions. HLE, METR, and *TheAgentCompany* help us separate these abilities before choosing a test for our own project.')
    text('These steps depend on each other. We will first make the smallest one clear: what evidence lets us call one run a success?')

    text('## 03 · What Is Task Success?')
    text('Our first task is simple: **cancel booking A and keep booking B**. Assume that the policy allows this cancellation. We want a scoring rule that another person could apply to the saved booking records.')
    text('Read the following outcomes as three possible runs of the same task. The request stays fixed. What changes is what the agent leaves behind.')
    text('- **The agent says it canceled A. Both bookings are still active.**\n\n  This run fails. The final message tells us what the agent claims, but the booking record shows that the requested change never happened.')
    text('- **The agent cancels both A and B.**\n\n  This run also fails. It made the requested change to A, but it also changed something the user explicitly wanted to keep. A check that only looks at A would miss this error.')
    text('- **The agent cancels A and keeps B active.**\n\n  This run passes our stated rule. The final state contains the requested change and preserves the other booking.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> Course synthesis grounded in tau squared benchmark task evaluation, ICML 2026</div>')
    text('We have separated what the agent says, what it tries, and what actually happens. **For this task, the booking state is the evidence that matters.**')
    text('One example gives us a rule. To test an agent across many requests, we also need tasks, tools, and a way to start each run from the same conditions. That is why we introduce a benchmark next.')

    text('## 04 · *τ²-Bench*')
    text('*τ²-Bench*, pronounced tau squared bench, turns customer support requests into tests that can be repeated. It gives an agent a simulated customer, company rules, and tools for reading or changing records.')
    text('**We use this benchmark to connect a success rule with an actual agent task.** It also lets us ask whether the agent can carry out the task repeatedly and work with a customer.')
    text('- **The task has a customer request and a policy.**\n\n  The request tells the agent what the customer wants. The policy tells it what actions are allowed. Both are needed to decide what a correct response should be.')
    text('- **The agent acts through tools.**\n\n  Retail tasks involve orders and returns. Airline tasks involve bookings. A useful answer often requires a change to a record, so we can inspect more than the final chat message.')
    text('- **In phone support, the customer can act too.**\n\n  For example, an agent may ask the customer to turn off airplane mode and report whether service returns. The agent must choose a fix and guide someone else through part of it.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_04_02.png"><img src="images/w12_04_02.png" alt="τ²-Bench · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> Barres et al. · ICML 2026 · PMLR 306, pp. 6736–6782</div></figure>')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_tau_setup.png"><img src="images/w12_tau_setup.png" alt="The support agent and simulated customer talk to each other and use their own tools."><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><figcaption class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a> Figure 1 from the authors’ <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2506.07982v1#S1.F1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">τ²-Bench preprint</a> · CC BY 4.0</figcaption></figure>')
    text("In the setup figure, follow the conversation and each side's tools. The two sides can change different parts of the environment. This is the reason the paper studies user coordination as well as the agent's own decisions.")
    text('Before studying coordination, we need to understand how a run is graded. The next airline task adds one detail to our booking example: the requested cancellation is not allowed.')

    text('## 05 · When an Agent Should Say No')
    text('Earlier, leaving booking A active meant failure because cancellation was allowed and requested. In this task from the *τ²-Bench Airline Tasks* repository, the policy does not allow the cancellation.')
    text('**The same unchanged booking can now be the correct result.** The agent should refuse the cancellation, explain why, and keep the booking. This is why we must read the task and policy before deciding what success looks like.')
    text('The original task record and the JSON excerpt below show how that expectation is recorded. Read them as instructions for the evaluator, not as a plan the agent should follow.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_05_02.png"><img src="images/w12_05_02.png" alt="Success Can Mean Refusing an Invalid Action · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-2" aria-label="Reference 2: τ²-Bench Airline Tasks">[2]</a> Sierra Research · Benchmark repository</div></figure><article id="ref-2" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_05_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[2] τ²-Bench Airline Tasks</h3><p class="reference-meta">Sierra Research · Benchmark repository</p><p>Task records and success criteria for the airline domain.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://github.com/sierra-research/tau2-bench/blob/main/data/tau2/domains/airline/tasks.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('<div class="source-code"><strong>Airline task 0 · JSON excerpt</strong><pre><code>{\n  &quot;id&quot;: &quot;0&quot;,\n  &quot;evaluation_criteria&quot;: {\n    &quot;actions&quot;: [],\n    &quot;communicate_info&quot;: [],\n    &quot;nl_assertions&quot;: [\n      &quot;Agent should refuse to proceed with the cancellation.&quot;\n    ],\n    &quot;reward_basis&quot;: [\n      &quot;DB&quot;,\n      &quot;COMMUNICATE&quot;\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>')
    text('- **DB and COMMUNICATE name parts of the check.**\n\n  DB refers to the database state. COMMUNICATE refers to information given to the user. Together they remind us to consider both what changed and what the customer was told.')
    text('- **nl_assertions lists checks in plain language.**\n\n  Here the assertion says the agent should refuse to proceed. That tells us why an empty list of expected actions can be consistent with successful behavior.')
    text('We can now explain both booking outcomes using their task rules. The next question is whether the agent follows the right rule every time we give it the same task.')

    text('## 06 · One Win or Every Run?')
    text('A correct refusal on one run does not tell us what happens on the next run. Reset the booking and repeat the request four times. Suppose the agent refuses correctly three times and cancels wrongly once.')
    text('Was that a success? We need to decide whether we are testing the chance of finding one good attempt or the chance of getting the task right on every attempt.')
    text('- **pass@k asks whether at least one of k attempts succeeds.**\n\n  This fits a setting where we can try several candidates and identify a correct one. A coding system with a trustworthy test may be able to use retries this way.')
    text("- **pass^k asks whether all k attempts succeed.**\n\n  This fits the concern that each customer should receive the correct result on their own run. One successful cancellation does not compensate for another customer's incorrect cancellation.")
    text('At k = 1, both ask about one attempt. As k increases, one successful attempt becomes easier to obtain, while a set of entirely successful attempts becomes harder.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> τ²-Bench, ICML 2026 (evaluation metrics)</div>')
    text('The Python example assumes independent runs, each with a 50% success chance. It lists all 16 possible outcomes for four runs. S means success and F means failure.')
    text('The any calculation counts outcomes with at least one S. The all calculation counts outcomes containing four S values. Use the buttons to see which outcomes each rule accepts.')
    outcomes = list(product((False, True), repeat=4))
    any_rate = sum(map(any, outcomes)) / len(outcomes)  # @inspect any_rate
    all_rate = sum(map(all, outcomes)) / len(outcomes)  # @clear any_rate @inspect all_rate
    text(outcome_demo(outcomes, any_rate, all_rate))  # @clear all_rate
    text('The results are 15/16, or 93.75%, and 1/16, or 6.25%. **The runs are the same; the success requirement changes the score.**')
    text("These are teaching numbers from a simple probability model. Next we will read the benchmark's measured results, where tasks have different levels of difficulty.")

    text('## 07 · Success Across Runs')
    text('We just compared two rules using invented outcomes. Now *τ²-Bench* asks how often real tested agents succeed on every run of a task. The figure reports pass^k across several models and support domains.')
    text('- **Start with one model and one domain.**\n\n  Each panel uses a different model. The colors represent retail, airline, and phone support. Keeping the panel and color fixed lets us examine repeated success without also changing the model or task domain.')
    text('- **Read from k = 1 to k = 4.**\n\n  At the left, one successful run counts. At the right, all four runs must succeed. We are making the requirement stricter, not giving the agent a different customer request.')
    text('- **Then compare the domains.**\n\n  This shows where the tested agent has more trouble meeting that requirement. It gives us a reason to examine one domain more closely on the next page.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_07_02.png"><img src="images/w12_07_02.png" alt="τ²-Bench: Reliability Across Repeated Runs · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> τ²-Bench, ICML 2026 (Figure 3, original experimental results)</div></figure><article id="ref-1" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_07_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[1] τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment</h3><p class="reference-meta">Barres et al. · ICML 2026 · PMLR 306, pp. 6736–6782</p><p>Repeated success and the effects of user coordination.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/barres26a.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('For GPT 4.1, phone support falls from 34% at k = 1 to 19% at k = 4. Retail falls from 74% to 53%, and airline support from 56% to 40% in this figure.')
    text('The 34% is an average across tasks. **It cannot simply be raised to the fourth power to obtain 19%.** The paper uses repeated runs of tasks; the previous calculation assumed the same success probability for every run.')
    text('Phone support is difficult in these results. Is the agent struggling to choose a fix, to guide the user, or both? A score alone cannot separate those possibilities, so the next experiment changes the setup.')

    text('## 08 · Who Controls the Tools?')
    text('Phone support involves two jobs: decide what should happen, then get the customer to carry out their part. The authors vary these demands to investigate where failures come from.')
    text('- **Default keeps the normal interaction.**\n\n  The agent and customer have separate tools. This includes both choosing a solution and explaining the required customer actions. It provides the starting point for the comparison.')
    text("- **No user gives the agent access to both sides' tools.**\n\n  The agent can perform the customer actions itself. Comparing this with Default helps test the cost of having to guide a separate user, although the interaction setup has also changed.")
    text('- **Oracle plan supplies the correct steps as well.**\n\n  Now the agent gets help with planning in the controlled setup. Comparing this with No user asks how much difficulty remains when the plan is provided.')
    text('The first chart uses the original policy. The second includes a workflow in the policy. Compare modes within one chart first, then ask what changes between the two policies.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_08_02.png"><img src="images/w12_08_02.png" alt="τ²-Bench: Reasoning and User Coordination · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_08_03.png"><img src="images/w12_08_03.png" alt="τ²-Bench: Reasoning and User Coordination · image 2"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> τ²-Bench, ICML 2026 (Figure 4, original Telecom ablation)</div></figure>')
    text('In the first chart, removing the need to guide the user improves both models, and supplying a plan helps further. The second chart shows why we should read each condition: extra guidance does not improve every result.')
    text('We have moved from counting failures to testing possible reasons for them. **The setup around the model is part of what we are evaluating.**')
    text('This raises a practical comparison problem. If we build a more elaborate agent, how do we know its improvement is worth the extra calls and cost? The next paper studies that question.')

    text('## 09 · *AI Agents That Matter*')
    text('*τ²-Bench* showed how repeated runs and task setup affect success. Now imagine that our new agent scores better after we add planning, retries, or several model calls. The score has improved, but we have also changed how much work the system is allowed to do.')
    text('**Does the design help, or does it mainly get more chances to succeed?** *AI Agents That Matter* makes this a central question in agent evaluation.')
    text("The paper examines cost, simple baselines, test sets, and repeatable comparisons. We use it here to move from 'my agent worked' to 'my agent is a useful improvement over another system.'")
    text('For our project, we would first compare with inexpensive ways to get extra attempts. Then we would check whether the result also holds on tasks that were kept separate during development.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_09_01.png"><img src="images/w12_09_01.png" alt="AI Agents That Matter · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> Kapoor et al. · TMLR 2025</div></figure>')
    text('The next section introduces those simple methods. Their role is to tell us how much improvement we can obtain before adding a more complicated agent design.')

    text('## 10 · Simple Baselines')
    text('Suppose a new coding agent gets several attempts, while the baseline gets only one. A higher score does not yet show whether the extra planning or tools helped. We need simple comparisons that can spend a similar budget.')
    text('- **Retry asks the same model again.**\n\n  One failed sample may be followed by a correct one. This checks how much improvement comes from having another chance. It only helps us choose a solution if we can check which attempts succeeded.')
    text('- **Warming increases the sampling temperature.**\n\n  Repeated attempts can produce very similar answers. Raising the temperature encourages more varied attempts. This tests whether a broader set of candidates helps, while still counting the extra calls.')
    text('- **Escalation switches to a stronger model after failure.**\n\n  The weaker model may handle easy tasks cheaply. A stronger model is tried when needed. This gives another way to spend the budget before building a more complex workflow.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> AI Agents That Matter, TMLR 2025; 2024 preprint (Sections 2.2 and 2.3)</div>')
    text('All three methods use resources. **Compare accuracy together with cost**, using the same tasks and clear stopping rules.')
    text('The next *HumanEval* result puts these choices on one plot. It lets us see whether extra cost buys enough additional correct answers.')

    text('## 11 · Accuracy and Cost on *HumanEval*')
    text('The paper tests these comparisons on *HumanEval*, which asks models to write short Python functions. The functions are checked with tests. The figure covers 164 problems and five runs.')
    text('We are reading this plot to choose between methods under a budget. Finding the highest point is only one part of that decision.')
    text('- **Read accuracy on the vertical axis.**\n\n  A higher point means more problems were solved in this evaluation. This is the benefit we are trying to obtain with extra attempts or a different agent design.')
    text('- **Read cost on the horizontal axis.**\n\n  The values use US dollars at April 2024 prices. Notice the axis break before comparing horizontal distances. Moving farther right means spending more for the reported result.')
    text('- **Compare the simple methods with agent methods.**\n\n  LDB means Large Language Model Debugger; LATS means Language Agent Tree Search. Their labels identify methods on the plot. We want to see whether a simpler method reaches a similar accuracy at lower cost.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_11_01.png"><img src="images/w12_11_01.png" alt="Accuracy and Cost on HumanEval · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> AI Agents That Matter, TMLR 2025; 2024 preprint (Figure 1; April 2024 prices; note the broken axes)</div></figure><article id="ref-3" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_11_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[3] AI Agents That Matter</h3><p class="reference-meta">Kapoor et al. · TMLR 2025</p><p>Cost, simple baselines, holdout sets, and reliable agent comparisons.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://openreview.net/forum?id=Zy4uFzMviZ" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01502" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View preprint ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('Several simple baselines do reach similar accuracy to more expensive methods in this comparison. Points near the upper left are attractive because they combine more correct answers with lower cost.')
    text("The Python example uses invented numbers: A solves 70% of tasks at 2 cents each, and B solves 80% at 9 cents. We filter the choices using a budget of 5 cents per task. Switch between All runs and 5¢ budget to see why B's higher accuracy may not make it usable.")
    runs = [('A', 0.70, 2), ('B', 0.80, 9)]  # accuracy and cents per task
    eligible = [(name, score) for name, score, cents in runs if cents <= 5]
    text(budget_demo([(name, score) for name, score, _ in runs], eligible))
    text('Cost tells us what we paid for the measured accuracy. It still does not tell us whether the method works beyond the tasks used to build it. Next we check what was kept separate for testing.')

    text('## 12 · Testing on New Tasks')
    text('Suppose we keep adjusting our booking agent after looking at its test failures. Its score may improve partly because we have built it around those examples. We need fresh evidence for a claim about future tasks.')
    text('Held-out cases are kept separate from development. **What we hold out must match what we claim will be new.** The booking examples below illustrate different sizes of claim.')
    text('- **New details within a familiar task.**\n\n  Change the passenger and booking while keeping the same kind of cancellation request. This checks whether the system can handle more instances of that task.')
    text('- **A new task in a familiar environment.**\n\n  Keep the support tools but ask for a different goal. This asks more than changing a name or booking number, so the test must include tasks that were not used to build the agent.')
    text('- **A new domain or environment.**\n\n  Move from booking support to a different kind of work. A broad claim needs evidence from that broader setting, because tool names, rules, and required actions may all change.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_12_02.png"><img src="images/w12_12_02.png" alt="Test Sets and Claims About Generalization · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> Kapoor et al. · TMLR 2025</div></figure>')
    text('The original table reviews 17 benchmarks. Read the claim category and the required holdout before the counts. The paper finds that many benchmarks did not have the kind of held-out test needed for their stated claim.')
    text('Use the three buttons to revisit what changes in our booking example. The point is to name the new element precisely when describing a test set.')
    text(story(STORIES[12][0], *STORIES[12][1]))
    text('We now have a success rule, a cost comparison, and a reason for fresh test cases. Before accepting the resulting score, we should also inspect failures and check whether the evaluation ran as intended.')

    text('## 13 · Why Did the Run Fail?')
    text('A failure is useful evidence only if we understand what failed. An agent may send the wrong tool arguments. A correct tool call may also fail because the service is unavailable. A grader may misread a successful result.')
    text('**Use the saved run to separate these causes.** This is how the earlier discussion becomes a practical check on our own experiment.')
    text('- **Find the first incorrect step.**\n\n  Read the task and tool calls in order. If the wrong booking ID appears early, the final failure has a different explanation from a service outage after a correct request.')
    text('- **Check the environment and the grader.**\n\n  Read tool responses and inspect the final state. If the state is correct but the score is a failure, investigate the scoring rule before changing the agent.')
    text("- **Repeat the run and record what changes.**\n\n  One unusual outcome may not describe the system's usual behavior. Repeated runs help us see whether the same mistake returns or whether the result varies.")
    text('- **Keep the comparison conditions clear.**\n\n  Use comparable tasks, tool access, and budgets for the two systems. Otherwise a score difference may reflect the experiment setup as well as the agent design.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> AI Agents That Matter, TMLR 2025; 2024 preprint (Section 6); practical synthesis</div>')
    text('So far, we have usually assumed that a record or a test can establish success. What if our agent writes an explanation and several answers are reasonable? We now need to examine who decides which result is better.')

    text('## 14 · Votes, Judges, and Outcomes')
    text('Our booking task had a state we could inspect. Our coding task had tests we could run. Now imagine that the agent explains a paper to a user. Two explanations may both be correct, while one is clearer or more useful.')
    text('**The way we grade a result should follow the question we want to answer.** Keep these three kinds of evidence separate as we move into rankings.')
    text('- **A direct check asks whether the required outcome occurred.**\n\n  For the booking task, inspect the booking record and policy. This tells us whether the task was completed under its rules.')
    text("- **A human vote asks which answer a person prefers.**\n\n  For two explanations, a reader may value clarity, detail, or style. That preference is useful information when we want to understand the user's experience.")
    text('- **A model judge applies a grading instruction.**\n\n  It can compare answers or decide whether a task passed. Its decision depends on the evidence and criteria it receives, so the judge itself must be checked.')
    text('We will start with human votes in *Chatbot Arena*. After seeing how votes become a ranking, we will ask whether a model can do some of this grading for us.')

    text('## 15 · *Chatbot Arena*')
    text('*Chatbot Arena* provides a way to collect the human preferences we just discussed. A user sends a prompt and sees answers from two models whose names are hidden. The user then votes for the answer they prefer.')
    text("**We introduce Arena because some useful comparisons need human judgment.** A request such as 'explain this paper to a beginner' allows several good answers. Comparing two answers can be easier than inventing an exact numerical score for each.")
    text('Many votes are combined into model scores. This allows prompts chosen by users to contribute to the evaluation, rather than relying only on a fixed set of questions.')
    text('For our project, such a score could help us choose a model for writing explanations. It would not by itself show that our booking tools work correctly. The task and the evidence have changed.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_15_01.png"><img src="images/w12_15_01.png" alt="Chatbot Arena · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</div></figure>')
    text("Before looking at the voting interface, we will make this change explicit: when do we need a known correct answer, and when do we want a user's preference?")

    text('## 16 · Right Answers and User Votes')
    text("If a math problem has a known solution, we can compare the model's answer with it. If the task is to write an explanation, people may agree that two answers are correct but still prefer one.")
    text('The figure compares several evaluation approaches. These names are examples of task and grading choices; we are using them to explain why Arena adds a different kind of evidence.')
    text('- ***MMLU* and *GSM8K* use questions with checkable answers.**\n\n  *MMLU* means Massive Multitask Language Understanding and covers knowledge across subjects. *GSM8K* contains grade school math problems. Their scores tell us about performance on the questions in those sets.')
    text('- ***HellaSwag* checks a different kind of answer.**\n\n  It tests commonsense completion. This is another reminder that even a score based on correct answers depends on the kind of question being asked.')
    text('- ***MT Bench* and *AlpacaEval* examine responses to instructions.**\n\n  *MT Bench* uses conversations with multiple turns. *AlpacaEval* compares answers to instructions. Such evaluations need a way to judge the quality of a response, not only to match a short answer.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_16_01.png"><img src="images/w12_16_01.png" alt="Benchmarks for Correctness and Human Preference · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</div></figure>')
    text('Fixed tests can become less informative if questions have appeared in training or if many models already score near the top. User comparisons provide another view, but that view depends on the users and prompts collected.')
    text("This explains the purpose of Arena's voting process. Next we will follow one comparison from the user's prompt to the vote that enters the ranking.")

    text('## 17 · Voting in *Chatbot Arena*')
    text('We know why a human comparison can be useful. Now we need to know what one Arena vote actually records. The original interface below shows the same prompt answered by two anonymous models.')
    text('- **The user reads both answers before seeing the model names.**\n\n  Hiding the names reduces the chance that the decision follows a brand or reputation. We want the vote to reflect the answers shown for this prompt.')
    text('- **The user chooses A, B, a tie, or that both are bad.**\n\n  The extra options matter. A comparison should allow the user to say that there is no clear winner or that neither answer is useful.')
    text("- **The vote describes this comparison.**\n\n  One user's choice does not describe every task or user. To estimate a broader preference, we need many comparisons and information about which model pairs received them.")
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_17_02.png"><img src="images/w12_17_02.png" alt="Chatbot Arena: Voting Rules · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</div></figure><article id="ref-4" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_17_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[4] Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference</h3><p class="reference-meta">Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</p><p>Blind pairwise comparisons and preference scores.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/chiang24b.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('Follow the interface in that order: prompt, answers, decision. The output of this process is a set of pairwise comparisons, not yet a single score for each model.')
    text('The next step is to combine those comparisons. We need a method that can handle many models, even when each model has faced a different set of opponents.')

    text('## 18 · From Votes to Scores')
    text('With just two models, six wins and four losses give A a 60% win share against B. A real leaderboard contains many models. A raw win share is harder to compare when the opponents differ.')
    text('The Bradley–Terry model combines pairwise results by assigning a score to each model. **A score difference represents a predicted chance of winning a comparison.**')
    text('- **Equal scores predict a 50% chance for either model.**\n\n  This provides the reference point. If the fitted model treats two systems as equally strong, it has no reason to favor either one in their comparison.')
    text('- **A larger score difference predicts a larger win chance.**\n\n  The scores summarize the observed comparisons. They are not percentages of tasks completed, so a leaderboard score cannot replace our booking success rate.')
    text('- **The formula links the scores to the recorded winner.**\n\n  In the original equation, ξ labels model scores and H records the comparison outcome. The method fits scores so that their predicted outcomes agree with the votes as well as possible.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_18_01.png"><img src="images/w12_18_01.png" alt="Chatbot Arena: Scores from Pairwise Votes · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</div></figure>')
    text('The Python example returns to just two models with no ties. It divides six wins by ten decisive votes. Use it to understand the win probability before thinking about a fit across many model pairs.')
    wins, losses = 6, 4
    fitted_probability = wins / (wins + losses)  # @inspect fitted_probability
    text(vote_demo(wins, losses, fitted_probability))  # @clear fitted_probability
    text('Which models faced each other and how many votes they received affect the evidence behind the ranking. With fewer comparisons, the ranking is less certain.')
    text('So far, we have asked how to turn recorded votes into scores. The next paper asks an earlier question: **which model versions get recorded and shown to the public?**')

    text('## 19 · *The Leaderboard Illusion*')
    text('Suppose one team enters one model and another privately tries twenty versions. If the second team publishes only the version with the highest score, the public table leaves out much of the testing process.')
    text('*The Leaderboard Illusion* examines private testing and selective reporting in *Chatbot Arena*. We introduce it here because a ranking depends on both the scoring formula and the choices made before a result becomes public.')
    text('**A fair scoring formula cannot reveal trials that were left out of the report.** To interpret the winning score, we also need to know how the reported version was selected.')
    text('This is relevant to our own agent project. If we try many prompts or settings and report only the best test score, the reader needs to know that selection happened.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_19_01.png"><img src="images/w12_19_01.png" alt="The Leaderboard Illusion · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5: The Leaderboard Illusion">[5]</a> Singh et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</div></figure>')
    text('We will first identify the private and public versions in the diagram. Then we will use a simulation to show why selection can raise the reported score even when the underlying average stays fixed.')

    text('## 20 · Which Models Get Reported?')
    text('The previous section raised a reporting question. The diagram makes that question visible: small dots represent privately tested variants, and the large dot represents the version made public.')
    text('- **The public result shows the selected version.**\n\n  A reader can compare that point with other public points. The plot alone does not reveal the full set of versions that were considered before selecting it.')
    text('- **More private trials create more opportunities to find a high score.**\n\n  Some differences may come from real changes, and some may come from variation in the test. The number of trials helps us understand how the winning result was obtained.')
    text('- **Reporting rules make the comparison easier to inspect.**\n\n  Record which versions were tested and why results were included or removed. This gives readers evidence about the selection process as well as the final number.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_20_02.png"><img src="images/w12_20_02.png" alt="Private Testing and Selective Score Reporting · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5: The Leaderboard Illusion">[5]</a> Singh et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</div></figure>')
    text('Look at the large dot, then look back at the small dots that led to it. This is the information that disappears when we see only the reported winner.')
    text('The diagram describes the process. To separate selection from real improvement, the next simulation keeps the underlying mean unchanged and varies how many versions are tried.')

    text('## 21 · Picking the Highest Score')
    text("We now want to see whether selecting the highest observed score can create an apparent improvement on its own. In the paper's simulation, the true mean stays at 1200 while the number of privately tested variants increases.")
    text('- **Read the number of variants on the horizontal axis.**\n\n  Moving right means more chances to obtain a high observed score. It does not mean that the simulation has raised the true mean.')
    text('- **Use the dashed line as the fixed reference.**\n\n  That line marks 1200. Comparing the reported result with it lets us see the effect of selection under this setup.')
    text('- **Follow the reported score as more variants are tested.**\n\n  The rising curve shows how a selected result can move upward even though the reference level stays the same. This is why the report should describe how the winner was chosen.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_21_01.png"><img src="images/w12_21_01.png" alt="Selecting the Best Variant Inflates the Score · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5: The Leaderboard Illusion">[5]</a> The Leaderboard Illusion, NeurIPS 2025 (Figure 7; simulation, not a live model ranking)</div></figure><article id="ref-5" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_21_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[5] The Leaderboard Illusion</h3><p class="reference-meta">Singh et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</p><p>Private testing and selective reporting of model variants.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/70a93f260a51123b3c0e33ecd1b4de97-Paper-Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('This simulation isolates a reporting effect. It does not show that every high score is misleading; it shows why a selected high score needs a fresh check.')
    text('The Python example uses five invented scores on a different scale. Report all variants shows the whole set. Report only the winner applies max and keeps 76%. The code makes the selection step explicit.')
    scores = [0.68, 0.73, 0.69, 0.76, 0.71]
    selected = max(scores)  # @inspect selected
    text(selection_demo(scores, selected))  # @clear selected
    text('After selecting a version, test it on fresh cases to see whether its lead remains. We can now return to the larger question: what kind of claim does a leaderboard score actually support?')

    text('## 22 · What Does the Score Tell Us?')
    text('We have followed human votes into a ranking and seen how reporting choices can affect the result. Before using that ranking to choose an agent, connect the score to the evidence that produced it.')
    text('- **Which answer did users prefer?**\n\n  Arena votes directly address this question for the users and prompts collected. They may help us choose a model for explaining ideas or answering open requests.')
    text('- **Did the agent finish the task?**\n\n  Return to our booking example. A preferred explanation does not show whether the booking changed correctly. That claim still needs the task outcome and its success rule.')
    text('- **Will it work on new tasks?**\n\n  A result on the tasks we have already tested does not settle this question. We need the held-out cases discussed earlier, chosen to match the intended claim.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chatbot Arena, ICML 2024; AI Agents That Matter, TMLR 2025; course synthesis</div>')
    text('**Use each score for the question its evidence can answer.** This keeps preference, completion, and performance on new tasks from getting mixed together.')
    text('Human comparisons are useful, but collecting them for every new project run can take substantial time. That practical cost motivates the next paper: can a model help us grade the results?')

    text('## 23 · LLMs as Judges')
    text('Suppose our project produces hundreds of explanations after every code change. Asking people to compare all of them would be slow. A model judge could make this check easier to run repeatedly.')
    text('A model judge receives a question, candidate answers, and grading instructions. It may choose a better answer or assign a score. **We now evaluate the grader as well as the system being graded.**')
    text('*Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena* compares model judgments with human judgments. It also studies biases such as favoring an answer because of its position or length.')
    text('The purpose of this paper in our lecture is to explain what changes when we replace some human decisions with model decisions. Lower grading effort is useful only if we understand what the grader gets right and wrong.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_23_01.png"><img src="images/w12_23_01.png" alt="Judging LLM as a Judge · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-6" aria-label="Reference 6: Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena">[6]</a> Zheng et al. · NeurIPS 2023</div></figure>')
    text('We will first inspect the input and rules given to the judge. Then we will test whether its decisions follow those rules consistently.')

    text('## 24 · A Judge Prompt')
    text('The judge is another model call. It cannot know our intended definition of a good answer unless the prompt and evidence make that definition clear. The original prompt below shows how the paper sets up a pairwise comparison.')
    text('- **Start with the question and the two answers.**\n\n  These tell the judge what was requested and what each candidate produced. Omitting part of the request can make an incomplete answer look acceptable.')
    text("- **Read the criteria for choosing the better answer.**\n\n  The criteria define what the judge should value. For our project, we need them to match the user's task, rather than rewarding an answer merely for being long or polished.")
    text('- **Check the requested output format.**\n\n  A fixed form for A, B, or a tie lets a program record the decision. It makes the output easier to use, although it does not guarantee that the decision is correct.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_24_02.png"><img src="images/w12_24_02.png" alt="The Default Pairwise Judge Prompt · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-6" aria-label="Reference 6: Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena">[6]</a> Zheng et al. · NeurIPS 2023</div></figure><article id="ref-6" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_24_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[6] Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena</h3><p class="reference-meta">Zheng et al. · NeurIPS 2023</p><p>Pairwise grading prompts and judge bias.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/91f18a1287b398d378ef22505bf41832-Abstract-Datasets_and_Benchmarks.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('Read the prompt as a scoring rule written in words. It tells us what the judge was asked to do and gives us something to inspect when a decision looks wrong.')
    text('A sensible prompt is a starting point. Next we need cases with known judgments so we can check whether the model actually follows the rule.')

    text('## 25 · Check Your Judge')
    text('We have specified the grading rule. Now we need evidence that the judge uses it. A simple check keeps the answers fixed and changes only their order.')
    text("- **Start with examples checked by people.**\n\n  These provide a reference for the judge's decisions. Include clear successes and clear failures so that agreement has an interpretable meaning.")
    text('- **Swap the positions of the same answers.**\n\n  If the judge follows the first position instead of the better answer, its decision depends on presentation. This is a useful check because the answer content has not changed.')
    text('- **Count two kinds of grading error separately.**\n\n  Passing a failed task can hide a broken agent. Failing a completed task can hide a useful improvement. A single agreement percentage can make these different problems hard to see.')
    text('- **Save the judge and its instructions.**\n\n  The model version, prompt, and scoring rule are part of the evaluation. If any of them changes, a different score may reflect the grader as well as the agent.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-6" aria-label="Reference 6: Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena">[6]</a> Judging LLM as a Judge with MT Bench and Chatbot Arena, NeurIPS 2023; practical synthesis</div>')
    text('The example deliberately uses a faulty judge that picks the first answer. Switch the order for a question with an obvious correct answer. This shows the error we are trying to detect; it is not a measured result from the paper.')
    text(story(STORIES[25][0], *STORIES[25][1]))
    text('So far, the judge has compared answer text. Our opening question was about agents that act in the world. To grade those agents, we may need screenshots or state records as evidence. The next paper makes that move.')

    text('## 26 · Grading Digital Agents')
    text('Return to the agent that says it finished a booking task. Grading the wording of its message would miss the central question: did the requested change happen? For an agent using an app or website, the screens and action history may provide evidence of the result.')
    text('*Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents* studies automated evaluation of these tasks. Its evaluators use information such as instructions and screenshots to judge completion.')
    text("**The input to the judge must show the work we want it to grade.** This is why we move from comparing two written answers to checking an agent's interaction with an interface.")
    text('We will follow a task that stops before producing the requested answer, compare two ways to read screenshots, and then see how an outcome check can disagree with action matching.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_26_01.png"><img src="images/w12_26_01.png" alt="Autonomous Evaluation of Digital Agents · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7: Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents">[7]</a> Pan et al. · COLM 2024</div></figure>')
    text("First, look at the actual request in the paper's example. A related page on the screen is not enough if the user asked for a specific piece of information.")

    text('## 27 · Grading from Screenshots')
    text("The task is to find the person who made the most commits to a project. The agent reaches a commits page, but its final response says it cannot determine the answer. The requested output is a person's name.")
    text('This example matters because the action can look reasonable. Opening the right kind of page is progress, but it does not complete the information request.')
    text('- **Read the instruction before judging the screen.**\n\n  The instruction tells us what evidence to look for. Here we need an identified person, not just proof that the agent visited a commits page.')
    text("- **Compare the visible progress with the final response.**\n\n  The agent's response does not supply the requested name. The evaluator uses the task and evidence together to judge the run as a failure.")
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_27_01.png"><img src="images/w12_27_01.png" alt="Judging Task Completion from Screenshots · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7: Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents">[7]</a> Pan et al. · COLM 2024</div></figure>')
    text('Follow the original example from the instruction to the response and the verdict. It applies the same principle as our booking check: judge the requested result, not just a plausible intermediate action.')
    text('This tells us what the grader should establish. Next we ask how to build a grader that can use screenshot evidence.')

    text('## 28 · Two Ways to Read Screenshots')
    text('Screenshots contain visual information that a text-only judge cannot read directly. The paper compares two routes from the task and screenshots to a completion judgment. VLM means vision language model; LM means language model.')
    text('- **A modular grader first describes the screenshots in text.**\n\n  A language model then grades the task using that description. This separates reading the image from making the decision, so we can inspect the description when investigating an error.')
    text('- **An end to end grader reads the images and text together.**\n\n  A model that accepts both makes the judgment directly. It avoids relying on a separate written description, but we still need to measure whether its decisions agree with human checks.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_28_02.png"><img src="images/w12_28_02.png" alt="Modular and End to End Evaluators · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7: Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents">[7]</a> Pan et al. · COLM 2024</div></figure><article id="ref-7" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_28_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[7] Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents</h3><p class="reference-meta">Pan et al. · COLM 2024</p><p>Compare action matching with checks of completed tasks.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://openreview.net/forum?id=NPAQ6FKSmK" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a><a href="https://nlp.cs.berkeley.edu/pubs/Pan-Zhang-Tomlin-Zhou-Levine-Suhr_2024_Autonomous_paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paper PDF ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('Follow both paths to the verdict in the original figure. To see why the difference matters, imagine that a screenshot contains an order confirmation. If the modular route leaves that detail out of its description, the next model may not have enough evidence to recognize success.')
    text('This example explains a possible source of error. Choosing a design still requires checking its grading accuracy and cost on the tasks we care about.')
    text('Both designs try to judge task completion. The next result compares that goal with a simpler scoring approach: checking how closely the agent followed a recorded sequence of actions.')

    text('## 29 · Matching Actions vs. Finishing')
    text("A recorded human path offers an easy reference for an app task. We can compare the agent's clicks and actions with that path. But a different path may reach the same goal, while a similar path may stop before finishing.")
    text("The paper's result uses *Android in the Wild*, or AitW, where agents follow instructions in phone apps. We use the figure to ask whether action matching and completed tasks give the same picture of performance.")
    text('- **Read the numbers in parentheses as action matching scores.**\n\n  These describe agreement with reference actions. They tell us about the path the agent took, which may be useful for analysis but does not directly establish the final outcome.')
    text('- **Read the bars as task completion judgments.**\n\n  Human and model graders assess whether tasks were completed. Comparing these bars with the action scores shows why the definition of the metric matters.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_29_01.png"><img src="images/w12_29_01.png" alt="Action Matching Can Give the Wrong Ranking · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7: Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents">[7]</a> Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents, COLM 2024 (Figure 2)</div></figure>')
    text('The measures give different pictures of performance. Before treating a higher score as a better agent, ask whether the metric rewards the thing the user asked it to achieve.')
    text('In the checkout example, A matches 4/4 reference actions but leaves the item in the cart. B matches 2/4 and completes checkout by another route. Switch the scoring rule to see the ranking reverse. These are teaching numbers.')
    action_scores = [('Trace A', 4 / 4), ('Trace B', 2 / 4)]
    outcome_scores = [('Trace A', 0), ('Trace B', 1)]
    text(matching_demo(action_scores, outcome_scores))
    text("**This returns us to the opening booking example.** Saying 'done,' taking plausible actions, and reaching the required final state are different pieces of evidence.")
    text("We now know why outcomes and graders must match the task. The next part asks which tasks to include when we want to make a broader claim about an agent's abilities.")

    text('## 30 · Differences Between Benchmarks')
    text('We have spent the last section making the grading rule match the task. Now suppose that our grader works well. A good score still tells us only about the work we actually tested.')
    text('**The task set determines which ability the score can describe.** We will compare three kinds of work because an agent project may need all three, while a benchmark may cover only one.')
    text('- **Answer a difficult question.**\n\n  HLE checks answers to expert questions. This helps us study knowledge and reasoning, but the task does not by itself require the agent to organize and complete a longer project.')
    text('- **Complete software work of different lengths.**\n\n  METR asks how success changes on tasks that take humans different amounts of time. This adds a question about the length of work the system can complete.')
    text('- **Finish work across several office tools.**\n\n  *TheAgentCompany* puts requests into a simulated workplace. This adds tool use, information gathering, and intermediate steps across applications.')
    text('We will read each project using the same questions: what work is given, how is success checked, and what claim follows? Start with the first case, where the final result is an answer to a hard question.')

    text("## 31 · *Humanity's Last Exam*")
    text('Earlier, we noted that a fixed test can become less useful when many models score near the top. If we want to compare their ability on harder questions, we need a test that leaves room to see differences.')
    text("*Humanity's Last Exam*, or HLE, was built for this purpose. Experts contribute questions across areas such as math, science, and the humanities. Each question has a known answer that can be checked.")
    text('**HLE adds difficult questions, not a full agent workflow.** Its role in this lecture is to show what a strong test of one ability can tell us, and where we would need additional tasks.')
    text('For a research assistant, this could be relevant to answering a technical question. It would not alone establish that the same assistant can find sources, run an experiment, and verify the resulting claim.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_31_01.png"><img src="images/w12_31_01.png" alt="Humanity&#x27;s Last Exam · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8: A benchmark of expert-level academic questions to assess AI capabilities">[8]</a> Center for AI Safety, Scale AI &amp; HLE Contributors Consortium · Nature 649, 1139–1146 (2026)</div></figure>')
    text('A difficult question must still be clear and have an answer we can check. Before reading an HLE accuracy, we look at how the question set was built.')

    text('## 32 · How HLE Was Built')
    text("A low model score could come from hard questions, but it could also come from unclear wording or incorrect answers in the test. HLE's construction process tries to separate these possibilities before evaluating models.")
    text('- **Experts propose questions and solutions.**\n\n  The answer and reasoning provide material for review. The goal is to create questions whose difficulty comes from the subject matter, not from an unexplained expectation.')
    text('- **A model difficulty check screens the questions.**\n\n  Questions that the tested models can already answer do not serve the same purpose as questions beyond their current performance. This step helps target the harder part of the question pool.')
    text('- **Experts review and refine the candidates.**\n\n  Reviewers check the answers and wording. This matters because an incorrect reference answer can make a correct model response appear wrong.')
    text('- **The project separates public and private questions.**\n\n  The public benchmark contains 2,500 questions. Keeping a private set provides additional questions that are not all exposed through repeated public testing.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_32_02.png"><img src="images/w12_32_02.png" alt="HLE: Building the Question Set · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8: A benchmark of expert-level academic questions to assess AI capabilities">[8]</a> Center for AI Safety, Scale AI &amp; HLE Contributors Consortium · Nature 649, 1139–1146 (2026)</div></figure><article id="ref-8" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_32_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[8] A benchmark of expert-level academic questions to assess AI capabilities</h3><p class="reference-meta">Center for AI Safety, Scale AI &amp; HLE Contributors Consortium · Nature 649, 1139–1146 (2026)</p><p>Humanity&#x27;s Last Exam and its expert question review process.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09962-4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14249" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View preprint ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('Follow the original diagram from contributions through screening and review to the final sets. The counts describe different stages of the process; they are not all counts of final test questions.')
    text('We have checked how the questions were selected. Now we need to know what help the model received when answering them, because the run settings also affect the score.')

    text('## 33 · What HLE Can Tell Us')
    text('Suppose two systems report different HLE accuracies. Before choosing between them, check whether they answered the questions under comparable conditions. This returns to the fair comparison problem from *AI Agents That Matter*.')
    text('- **State which tools the model can use.**\n\n  Browsing or running code can change how a question is solved. A result with tools and a result from the model alone describe different tested systems.')
    text('- **State the number of attempts and how the answer is chosen.**\n\n  One attempt and several attempts provide different opportunities for success. This is the same distinction we introduced with pass@k and repeated runs.')
    text('- **Check reported confidence against actual correctness.**\n\n  If a set of answers receives 80% confidence, about 80% should be correct for that confidence to be well calibrated. Confidence is useful only if it helps us judge how often the answer is right.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8: A benchmark of expert-level academic questions to assess AI capabilities">[8]</a> Humanity&#x27;s Last Exam, Nature 2026; arXiv 2501.14249 (scope and limitations)</div>')
    text('An HLE score describes answers to these questions under these settings. **To claim that an agent can finish a longer workflow, test a workflow.**')
    text('This motivates a study by METR, a group that evaluates AI systems. It changes the question from answering difficult items to completing software tasks that take people different amounts of time.')

    text('## 34 · Long Software Tasks')
    text('A research or coding assistant may answer one question correctly and still lose track of a task that takes many steps. We need a way to ask how success changes as the amount of work increases.')
    text('*Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks* uses human completion time as a reference. Some tasks take a person a minute; others take much longer. The study measures agent success on tasks across that range.')
    text('**The purpose is to describe task length alongside success.** An overall accuracy would mix short and long tasks together and could hide where the agent starts to struggle.')
    text("For our project, this approach helps distinguish success on a quick fix from success on work that takes sustained effort. Human task time is the reference used in this study, so we must keep it separate from the agent's own running time.")
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_34_01.png"><img src="images/w12_34_01.png" alt="Long Software Tasks · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9: Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks">[9]</a> Kwa et al. · NeurIPS 2025</div></figure>')
    text('Next we will see how human timings and agent outcomes are collected, then how they are combined into a time horizon.')

    text('## 35 · What Is a Time Horizon?')
    text('METR stands for Model Evaluation and Threat Research. The study needs two pieces of information about a task: how long a human takes and whether the agent completes it. Neither piece answers the full question alone.')
    text('- **Measure human task duration.**\n\n  People complete the tasks so the study can estimate their length. This supplies a common reference for comparing agent results across tasks.')
    text("- **Record agent success or failure on the tasks.**\n\n  The agent attempts the work and is graded using the task's success condition. We now have outcomes that can be compared with the human timing information.")
    text('- **Fit a curve relating success to human task time.**\n\n  Individual runs vary, so the fitted curve summarizes the pattern across tasks. We can then ask what task duration corresponds to a chosen probability of success.')
    text('The task sets include HCAST, Human Calibrated Autonomy Software Tasks; SWAA, Software Atomic Actions; and *RE Bench*, Research Engineering Benchmark. They supply software work at different task lengths.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_35_01.png"><img src="images/w12_35_01.png" alt="METR: Estimating the Time Horizon · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9: Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks">[9]</a> Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks, NeurIPS 2025 (Figure 2)</div></figure>')
    text('A 50% time horizon is the human task duration where the fitted agent success rate reaches 50%. It summarizes one point on this curve. It does not mean that every task of that duration will be completed.')
    text('We have defined the measurement. Next we will read one reported curve and see how choosing a different target success rate changes the time we read from it.')

    text('## 36 · Success by Task Length')
    text("The original panel shows Claude 3.7 Sonnet in the paper's setup. The horizontal axis is human completion time. The vertical axis is the agent's chance of success. We use this result to practice interpreting a time horizon.")
    text('- **Find 50% on the vertical axis.**\n\n  Move across to the fitted curve. This selects the success rate we want to summarize, rather than selecting the longest task the model ever happened to finish.')
    text("- **Read the corresponding human task time.**\n\n  Move down from the intersection to the horizontal axis. The marked estimate is about 54 minutes in this result. That time describes the human reference for the task, not the agent's running time.")
    text('- **Try a higher success requirement.**\n\n  At 90% success, the intersection lies at a shorter task duration. The chosen probability matters, so report it whenever reporting a time horizon.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_36_01.png"><img src="images/w12_36_01.png" alt="METR: Success Probability and Human Task Time · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9: Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks">[9]</a> Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks, NeurIPS 2025 (Figure 4, selected panel)</div></figure><article id="ref-9" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_36_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[9] Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks</h3><p class="reference-meta">Kwa et al. · NeurIPS 2025</p><p>Relate task success to the time a human expert needs.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/85069585133c4c168c865e65d72e9775-Abstract-Conference.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text("The Python example draws an invented curve and finds its first sampled point at or below 50%. Its crossing is 30 minutes. Use Read the axes and Find 50% to practice the procedure; the paper's measured 54-minute result remains in the original figure.")
    minutes = list(range(0, 61, 5))
    probabilities = [1 / (1 + (t / 30) ** 1.4) for t in minutes]
    crossings = [t for t, p in zip(minutes, probabilities) if p <= 0.5]
    horizon = min(crossings)  # @inspect horizon
    text(horizon_demo(minutes, probabilities, horizon))  # @clear horizon
    text('We can now explain a time horizon using the task set, human timings, and success rule. The curve is only as meaningful as those inputs.')
    text('That leads to another evaluation problem: what if the coding task or its test is wrong? The next audit examines the benchmark itself rather than assuming every failed check is an agent failure.')

    text('## 37 · Checking Coding Tasks')
    text('Our earlier model judge could wrongly pass or fail a result. A coding test can make similar errors. It may reject a valid fix because of a hidden requirement, or accept an incomplete fix because its checks miss the problem.')
    text("**We must check the benchmark as well as the agent.** OpenAI's *Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations* audits the 731-task public split of *SWE-Bench Pro*.")
    text('The audit screens tasks using attempts and failure traces, then reviews flagged cases with agents and people. The original figure shows how those stages fit together.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_37_01.png"><img src="images/w12_37_01.png" alt="Codex: Auditing Coding Benchmark Tasks · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10: Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations">[10]</a> OpenAI, Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations, Jul 2026 (original methodology figure)</div></figure><article id="ref-10" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_37_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[10] Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations</h3><p class="reference-meta">OpenAI · 2026</p><p>Audit the tasks and tests behind software benchmark scores.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://openai.com/index/separating-signal-from-noise-coding-evaluations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('The agent-assisted review identified 200 broken tasks. The human campaign identified 249. These are results from two review processes, so do not add them to obtain a total.')
    text('Examples include hidden requirements, overly strict tests, and tests that miss incomplete fixes. A suspicious failure may therefore require repairing a task or test before drawing a conclusion about the model.')
    text('This audit brings us back to the need for saved evidence. The next section collects the records that would let another person investigate an evaluation result in our own project.')

    text('## 38 · What to Save')
    text("Imagine handing a teammate the statement 'our agent scored 70%.' They cannot yet tell what was counted, whether the comparison was fair, or how to investigate a mistake. The papers we have discussed tell us what to save alongside that number.")
    text('- **Save the tasks and success conditions.**\n\n  Include task IDs, inputs, and required outcomes. This lets someone check whether a pass means a correct answer, a preferred response, or a completed action.')
    text('- **Save the system and comparison settings.**\n\n  Record model and tool versions, the baseline, budgets, and attempt limits. These explain what was allowed to change when comparing the two systems.')
    text('- **Save runs and failure evidence.**\n\n  Keep tool responses, final states, and repeated runs. These help separate an agent mistake from an environment problem or an unusual attempt.')
    text('- **Save the grader and human checks.**\n\n  Record its prompt, version, and scoring rule, together with checked examples. This lets another person examine whether the score changed because the grader changed.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10: Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations">[10]</a> Practical synthesis from the research papers and the OpenAI July 2026 audit</div>')
    text('**A useful evaluation report connects the score to records that explain it.** These are practical outputs for a student project, not just extra details in a paper.')
    text('We will use one final workplace benchmark to bring the pieces together. It combines several tools and intermediate steps, making both the success rule and the saved evidence especially important.')

    text('## 39 · Optional: *TheAgentCompany*')
    text('So far, we have used bookings, written answers, app actions, and software tasks to examine parts of an evaluation. Real office work can combine several of these within one request.')
    text('*TheAgentCompany* puts agents in a simulated company with documents, chat, websites, and code. The agent receives a work request and uses the environment to complete it.')
    text('**This final case shows how our evaluation questions fit together in one workflow.** We need to define the whole task, inspect actions across tools, and distinguish partial progress from completion.')
    text('The benchmark can check intermediate milestones as well as the final result. That helps us see where work stopped, while keeping a separate question about whether the full request was completed.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_39_01.png"><img src="images/w12_39_01.png" alt="Optional: TheAgentCompany · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-11" aria-label="Reference 11: TheAgentCompany: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Consequential Real World Tasks">[11]</a> Xu et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</div></figure>')
    text('First we will look at the workplace and its tasks. Then we will read the results by tool and task type, rather than relying only on one overall score.')

    text('## 40 · Workplace Tasks')
    text('The benchmark contains 175 tasks. GitLab stores code, ownCloud holds files, RocketChat handles conversations, and Plane tracks projects and tasks. These tools create a setting where completing one request may require several kinds of action.')
    text('- **The request defines a piece of workplace work.**\n\n  Tasks include arranging a meeting, analyzing a spreadsheet, and preparing a code release. The agent needs to identify the required deliverable before deciding which tools to use.')
    text('- **The agent may gather information across tools and colleagues.**\n\n  Finding a document may be only the first step. It may also need to ask a simulated colleague for information or use that information in another application.')
    text('- **The evaluator checks outcomes and intermediate milestones.**\n\n  A milestone records useful progress toward the goal. It can help identify where the agent stopped, but it should not be confused with completing the full request.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_40_01.png"><img src="images/w12_40_01.png" alt="TheAgentCompany: Tasks Across Workplace Tools · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-11" aria-label="Reference 11: TheAgentCompany: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Consequential Real World Tasks">[11]</a> Xu et al. | NeurIPS 2025 | Figure 1 | Original benchmark design</div></figure><article id="ref-11" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_40_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[11] TheAgentCompany: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Consequential Real World Tasks</h3><p class="reference-meta">Xu et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</p><p>Workplace tasks that span tools and score intermediate milestones.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/0d744742f6fac4d1134c019b7cef3c8a-Paper-Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('Follow the original diagram from the workplace tools to the tasks and evaluation. As a teaching example, finding the source file for a report is a milestone; producing and saving the requested report is a later outcome.')
    text('This is why the next results need two kinds of reading: where the agent succeeds across different tools, and how the score treats partial progress.')

    text('## 41 · Scores by Task and Tool')
    text('A single workplace score can hide large differences between types of work. The original figure separates success by platform on the left and task category on the right. Each color identifies a tested model.')
    text('- **Compare platforms within the same model.**\n\n  This keeps the model fixed while changing the tool setting. For example, the tested systems do worse on ownCloud than on Plane in this figure. The platform can be part of what makes a task difficult.')
    text('- **Compare the kinds of workplace tasks.**\n\n  SDE means software development engineering, PM means project management, and DS means data science. Admin means administration and HR means human resources. These categories show why one overall result may not describe the work we care about.')
    text('- **Read full success and partial credit separately.**\n\n  Full success requires completing the task. A score with partial credit also rewards specified intermediate progress. The two numbers answer different questions about how far the agent got.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_41_01.png"><img src="images/w12_41_01.png" alt="TheAgentCompany: Results by Platform and Task · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-11" aria-label="Reference 11: TheAgentCompany: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Consequential Real World Tasks">[11]</a> Xu et al. | NeurIPS 2025 | Figure 2 | Tested model versions in the paper</div></figure>')
    text('Table 1 in the paper reports 30.3% full success and a 39.3% score with partial credit for OpenHands with Gemini 2.5 Pro. The larger number should not be read as the percentage of fully completed tasks.')
    text("This returns us to the booking and checkout examples. Finding the right page, taking useful actions, or finishing one milestone can be progress while the user's full request remains unfinished.")
    text('We have now applied the same questions to a complete workplace setting. The final section turns them into the argument we should be able to make when reporting our own agent results.')

    text('## 42 · Four Checks for a Score')
    text("At the start, we asked how to move from one convincing demo to a result someone else can check. Suppose the conclusion of our project is 'our agent is better than the baseline.' We should now be able to explain four parts of that claim.")
    text("- **What counted as success?**\n\n  Use the booking and checkout examples to separate messages, actions, and completed outcomes. The success rule should follow the user's request and the task's constraints.")
    text('- **Was the comparison fair?**\n\n  Use the same tasks and clear budgets, then report repeated attempts and how a version was selected. This addresses the cost and selection effects we saw in the papers.')
    text('- **Was the grading checked?**\n\n  Inspect the judge or test, use cases checked by people, and save failures. A higher score is informative only if we understand what the scoring process accepts.')
    text('- **Which tasks does the evidence cover?**\n\n  Explain what was held out and which kinds of work were tested. A question set, a software task suite, and a simulated workplace support different conclusions.')
    text('**A score becomes useful when we can explain the task, the comparison, the grader, and the scope of the claim.** The papers in this lecture each help fill in one of those parts.')

    text('## 43 · Thank you')
    text('Keep the same question when reading a new agent result: what evidence connects this score to the work the system is supposed to do?')
    text('The reference cards beside the relevant papers link to the original sources. Use them to check the figures, task rules, and evaluation settings discussed here.')
